Great question. In JavaScript, file upload usually has 3 parts:

- Let user pick a file with `<input type="file">`
- Put that file into a `FormData` object
- Send it to your backend with `fetch()`

```html
<input id="avatarInput" type="file" accept="image/*" />
<button id="uploadBtn">Upload</button>
<div id="status"></div>
```

```js
const API_BASE_URL = "http://localhost:8000"; // your API

document.getElementById("uploadBtn").addEventListener("click", async () => {
  const input = document.getElementById("avatarInput");
  const status = document.getElementById("status");
  const file = input.files?.[0];

  if (!file) {
    status.textContent = "Please choose a file first.";
    return;
  }

  // Optional client checks
  if (file.size > 5 * 1024 * 1024) {
    status.textContent = "File too large (max 5MB).";
    return;
  }

  const formData = new FormData();
  formData.append("file", file); // "file" must match backend field name

  try {
    const token = localStorage.getItem("authToken");

    const res = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/settings/profile-picture`, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: {
        Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` // do NOT set Content-Type manually
      },
      body: formData
    });

    const data = await res.json();
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(data.detail || "Upload failed");

    status.textContent = "Upload successful!";
    // e.g. data.file_url -> update profile picture preview
  } catch (err) {
    status.textContent = err.message;
  }
});
```

Key tips:
- Use `FormData` for files, not JSON.
- Don’t manually set `Content-Type`; browser sets correct `multipart/form-data` boundary.
- Backend must accept multipart uploads (for FastAPI, this is typically `UploadFile = File(...)`).
- Validate type/size on both frontend and backend.

